Repair only the supplied scientific or grammatical issues. Return {sections:[{id:number,patches:[{before:string,after:string,issueIndex:number}],reason:string}]}. Include each supplied section exactly once. Use exact unique substrings in its candidate. At most 3 non-overlapping patches per section; their combined BEFORE text must cover at most 35% of that section. issueIndex refers to that section's issues. Keep wording elsewhere unchanged. Restore the original meaning, uncertainty and proposal status. No extra style pass. If bounded repairs cannot solve the issues, return patches:[] and explain.
INPUT DATA:
{"sections": [{"id": 0, "heading": "Abstract", "original": "A timetable can lower average waiting time while leaving some journeys less reliable. We propose to compare a fixed timetable with a holding policy using an anonymized arrival log from one bus route. The study will examine mean waiting time alongside long waits, with peak and off-peak periods reported separately [S2]. A better overall average will not be treated as evidence that all passengers benefit.", "candidate": "A timetable can reduce average waiting time but may make some journeys less reliable. We propose comparing a fixed timetable with a holding policy using an anonymized arrival log from one bus route. The study will examine mean waiting time alongside the frequency of long waits, with peak and off-peak periods reported separately [S2]. A better overall average will not be treated as evidence that all passengers benefit.", "issues": [{"originalQuote": "while leaving some journeys less reliable", "revisedQuote": "but may make some journeys less reliable", "problem": "The revision introduces a claim that the timetable may cause reliability to deteriorate for some journeys. The original describes uneven reliability despite a lower average, without asserting such deterioration."}]}, {"id": 1, "heading": "Introduction", "original": "Passengers may care as much about an unusually long delay as they do about a typical journey. A single average can hide these differences. The available log records scheduled and observed arrivals on weekdays but includes no passenger identities [S1]. It can support a comparison of service patterns, although it cannot establish how individual passengers experienced those patterns.", "candidate": "Passengers may care equally about an unusually long delay as they do about a typical journey. A single average waiting time can obscure these differences. The available log records scheduled and observed arrivals on weekdays but includes no passenger identities [S1]. While it supports a comparison of service patterns, it cannot establish how individual passengers experienced those patterns.", "issues": [{"originalQuote": "may care as much about an unusually long delay as they do about a typical journey", "revisedQuote": "may care equally about an unusually long delay as they do about a typical journey", "problem": "“Equally about ... as they do about” is an awkward comparative construction. Retain “as much about ... as” or use “equally about ... and ...”."}]}, {"id": 3, "heading": "Methodology", "original": "We will apply the fixed timetable and holding policy to arrivals from 18 weekdays [S2]. Each simulated decision will use only information available at that point in the log. Peak and off-peak periods will remain separate throughout analysis. We will preserve instances of service disruption, since removing difficult days could make the policy appear more dependable than it is.", "candidate": "We will apply the fixed timetable and holding policy to arrivals from 18 weekdays [S2]. Each simulated decision will use only information available at that point in the log. Peak and off-peak periods will remain separate throughout analysis. To ensure a realistic assessment, we will preserve instances of service disruption, as removing difficult days could make the policy appear more dependable than it is.", "issues": [{"originalQuote": "We will preserve instances of service disruption, since removing difficult days could make the policy appear more dependable than it is.", "revisedQuote": "To ensure a realistic assessment, we will preserve instances of service disruption, as removing difficult days could make the policy appear more dependable than it is.", "problem": "“Ensure a realistic assessment” adds an unsupported assurance. Preserving disruptions reduces a potential bias but does not ensure realism, especially given the manuscript’s stated limitations of historical replay."}]}, {"id": 4, "heading": "Planned Evaluation", "original": "The planned measures are mean waiting time, the frequency of long waits and the difference between peak and off-peak outcomes. We will report days on which the holding policy performs worse as well as those on which it improves the average. Sensitivity checks will vary the definition of a long wait without selecting the definition that most favours either policy.", "candidate": "The planned measures include mean waiting time, the frequency of long waits, and the difference between peak and off-peak outcomes. We will report days when the holding policy performs worse as well as those where it improves the average. Sensitivity checks will vary the definition of a long wait without favouring either policy.", "issues": [{"originalQuote": "Sensitivity checks will vary the definition of a long wait without selecting the definition that most favours either policy.", "revisedQuote": "Sensitivity checks will vary the definition of a long wait without favouring either policy.", "problem": "The revision removes the explicit commitment against selecting the most favourable definition. A general promise of neutrality does not preserve this concrete safeguard against selective reporting."}]}], "sources": [{"id": "S1", "title": "Controlled source packet: domain and evidence brief", "excerpt": "The proposed transport study uses an anonymized bus arrival log from one route. The log contains scheduled and observed arrivals but no passenger identities. Its coverage is limited to weekdays. A lower mean waiting time does not imply that every passenger group benefits.", "content_kind": "author_constructed_evaluation_fixture"}, {"id": "S2", "title": "Controlled source packet: experimental design brief", "excerpt": "The team will compare a fixed timetable with a holding policy using the same recorded arrivals. Peak and off-peak periods will be reported separately. The analysis will use 18 weekdays and will not make a causal claim about passenger satisfaction.", "content_kind": "author_constructed_evaluation_fixture"}]}
